","PETITIONERs are the heirs of a recorded owner who say the opposite party had been permitted to remain on land he had already sold to them for Tk. 27,00,000 only until he could find somewhere else to live, and that he later declined to move despite a lawyer's notice calling on him to hand over possession. RESPONDENT is the recorded owner himself, who had earlier sold the land to them and then sued for recovery of possession, treating the permission as revoked. The revisional court acceded to his prayer to bring in other parties, and the heirs question whether the suit can proceed at all without first settling whether the vendor is alive and of sound mind.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the central question in the suit had been left untouched by both courts below, namely whether the original defendant is alive, and if so whether he is of unsound mind and incapable of defending the suit, which affects the validity of everything done in it. Instead of addressing that, the revisional judge had acceded to an application for addition of parties. The orders were effaced and the trial court was told to hold a neutral enquiry, take additional evidence and dispose of the suit within a year.",2e99ff1fdf9c995f76a99a0708d2b21e58058ddc26cfc12420b812454fa511bb
