","PETITIONERs are the purchasers of land at Companiganj in Noakhali against whom a pre-emption case under section 96 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act was brought by a neighbour who claimed to be both a co-sharer by purchase and the owner of adjoining land. The Assistant Judge threw the case out, but the Additional District Judge acceded to it on appeal, and the purchasers now question that reversal. RESPONDENTs are the pre-emptor and the vendors. Nobody appeared for either side at the hearing.","Other: State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950",REFUSED,"The court went through the khatians and the pleadings and found that the pre-emptor held land contiguous to the plot sold and that the co-sharers of the same diara khatian had been impleaded, so that the objection that the case was defective for want of parties could not be sustained. The sale had not been notified to him, and once he came to know of it he brought the case within time and observed the required formalities. The appellate order restoring the case was therefore left undisturbed.",6cbbf3b77f9beebd0abf854e9e2750071e6c41d4b6a4cc591f33f1cc25a9f6eb
