","PETITIONER was convicted under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 over a cheque for Tk. 42,00,000 drawn on a bank at Lalmonirhat, which was dishonoured for want of funds, and his appeal was turned down by the Sessions Judge. RESPONDENT is the complainant. In revision he says that the money has since been paid in full and that the parties have composed their differences, so that the conviction, the term of imprisonment and the fine should not continue to hang over him any longer.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",ALLOWED,"The court observed that the purpose of the section is the recovery of the amount of the cheque, and that the complainant had received what was due; both sides confirmed an amicable settlement leaving no subsisting claim. Once the money had been paid, there was no purpose in keeping a term of imprisonment and a fine alive, and the court found legal ground to step in. The judgment of the appellate court and the sentence were effaced, the petitioner was acquitted, and the deposited sum was released to the complainant.",bf57102deedb3d68886812f69b4de3b0df7c1f5d4876bc10813e3490aae100cc
