","PETITIONER is a borrower who took a loan of over Tk. 62,00,000 from a bank at Cox's Bazar and gave fifteen cheques towards repayment, all of which were dishonoured in 2012 for want of funds, leading to his conviction under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881. RESPONDENTs are the state and the bank. He appeals against the year's imprisonment and the fine, but the bank has meanwhile been paid the whole amount due under a settlement between the parties.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",ALLOWED,"The court recorded that the complainant bank, having received the cheque amount under an amicable settlement, no longer pressed for punishment, and that the object of the provision had been achieved. Keeping the appellant in prison after the debt had been satisfied would serve no purpose in a case of this kind. The conviction and sentence of the trial court were effaced, the appellant was acquitted, and the sum deposited at the appellate stage was released to the bank.",6401ed7439661536204b85baba2db16ae145505396f34cc7af6790517769e8bf
