","PETITIONER is a complainant who had spent large sums on a project promised by an accused at Chuadanga, leading to the conviction of that accused under section 420 of the Penal Code with three years and a fine, the conviction being sustained in appeal. RESPONDENT is the accused, and the state is the other party. The complainant comes in revision because the parties have since reached a private settlement and the accused has returned the money, so that he has no further interest in seeing the sentence carried out against him any longer.","Other: Penal Code, 1860",ALLOWED,"The court found that the accused had made good the amount claimed and that the parties had compromised their dispute, the complainant stating before the court that he had no further claim and did not oppose relief, the state offering no objection. Once the offence had been compounded in substance and the loss made good, the punitive consequence could not usefully be maintained. The judgment of the appellate court and the conviction were effaced, the accused was acquitted, and the matter was closed without costs.",3ba4958747141582f8604cb259e5cdc652f0138ec21e79e432562228db732c10
