","PETITIONERs are plaintiffs in a long running partition suit at Bheramara in Kushtia over a small parcel of homestead land, whose suit was decreed in part and then modified on appeal. The defendants carry a cross revision against the refusal to give one of them a larger share. Both rules turn on how the shares in the plot were worked out, whether a co-defendant who did not appeal could still be given a separate share, and whether adjoining plots could be bought up under section 4 of the Partition Act.","Other: Partition Act, 1897",PARTLY,"The court held that in a partition suit a co-defendant who has not appealed may still receive a separate share, since the court must avoid multiplicity of proceedings and the parties stand on the same footing, and that the share allotted to the second defendant had been wrongly cut down. It found, however, that the adjoining plots were commercial land in a town, not an undivided dwelling house, so that the ingredients of section 4 were absent. Both rules were thus answered in part.",a98b012ed16c81eaf485db90f8ced2d63fc674e048c13177cf496075da43c8b9
