3850-3889,Criminal Appeal No. 5289 of 2018,2024-06-04,"
<<PAGE 1>>
Present 
Mr. Justice Sheikh Abdul Awal 
Criminal Appeal No. 5289 of 2018 
 
Md. Mozammel @ Muju and others 
 
 
  .............Convict-appellants. 
-Versus- 
      The State  
 
                                                              ............Respondent. 
                          No one appears 
       .....For the convict-appellants. 
                          Ms. Shahida Khatoon, D.A.G with  
                          Ms. Sabina Perven, A.A.G with, 
                          Ms. Koheenoor Akter, A.A.G. 
 
 
 
                       ............. For the respondent.  
 
 
                                           Judgment on 04.06.2024. 
Sheikh Abdul Awal, J: 
This criminal appeal at the instance of convict 
appellant Nos. 1. Md. Mozammel @ Muju, 2. Md. Billal 
Mollah and 3. Md. Alam Hossain is directed against the 
impugned judgment and order of conviction and Sentence 
dated 15.11.2017 passed by the learned Additional Sessions 
Judge 2nd Court, Gazipur in Sessions Case No. 209 of 2013  
arising out of  G.R. No. 801 of 2012 corresponding to 
Joydevpur Police Station case No. 117 dated 31.07.2012 
convicting the accused appellants under table 22(Ga) of 
the Madok Drabbya Niyantron Ain, 1990 and sentencing 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2
him thereunder to suffer rigorous  imprisonment for a 
period of 2(two) years and to pay a fine of Taka 5,000/-
(three thousand) in default to suffer simple imprisonment 
for 01 (one) month more.  
No one appears for the convict appellant despite of 
fact that this matter has been appearing in the list for 
hearing with the name of the learned Advocate for the 
appellants for days together.  
In view of the fact that this petty old criminal 
Appeal arising out of 2(two) years sentence has been 
dragging before this Court for more than 5 years, I am 
inclined to dispose of the same. 
 
At the time of pronouncement of judgment, it 
appears from the record that this Court on 29.05.2018 at 
the time of admission of appeal granted ad-interim bail 
to the accused appellant for a period of 01(one) year, 
which was lastly extended on 15.05.2019 for a period of 
01(one) year and thereafter, no one took any steps to 
extend the order of bail, as a result of which the said bail 
was expired long before on 15.05.2020. In that view of 
the matter, I have no hesitation to hold that the convict 
appellants are fugitive from law and justice. 
In the case of Anti-Corruption Commission Vs. Dr. 
HBM Iqbal Alamgir, reported in 15 BLC(AD) 44, it has 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3
been held that the Court would not act in aid of an 
accused person, who is a fugitive from law and justice. 
In view of the above, this Appeal must fail. 
In the result, the appeal is dismissed. Since the 
appeal is dismissed the convict- appellant Nos. 1. Md. 
Mozammel @ Muju, 2. Md. Billal Mollah and 3. Md. Alam 
Hossain are directed to surrender their bail bond within 3 
(three) months from today to suffer their sentence, 
failing which the Trial Court below shall take necessary 
steps to secure arrest against the convict appellants.  
 
Send down the lower Court records at once. 
 
","PETITIONERs are three men convicted by an Additional Sessions Judge at Gazipur in 2017 under table 22(Ga) of the narcotics statute for keeping contraband, and sentenced to two years with a fine. RESPONDENT is the state. No one appeared for them or advanced any argument on their behalf although the appeal has been on the list for days, and the interim bail conferred at admission expired in 2020 without anyone seeking its extension, so the court treats them as having absconded.","Other: Narcotics Control Act, 1990",REFUSED,"The court noted that the appellants had been given ad interim bail for a year in 2018, that it was last extended in 2019, and that from 2020 nobody took any step to keep it alive, which showed that they had removed themselves from the process of law. Relying on the settled principle that a court will not assist a person who is a fugitive from justice, and finding nothing on the record to disturb the conviction, the court declined to hear the appeal on its merits and required them to surrender within three months.",0c8fe510c6686a01cbdb7275a421737dac3f1293ed7d9bff359fe473262f5bf5
