","PETITIONER holds a two-year contract with the Chattogram Port Authority for jetty handling, cleaning and garbage removal, and complains that the authority has been deducting value added tax at fifteen per cent from his monthly bills instead of the reduced rate applicable to his kind of service. RESPONDENTs are the port authority and the revenue. He asks that the deduction from his running bills be declared unlawful and that the excess so collected from him be refunded with effect from the first bill raised under the contract.","Other: Value Added Tax and Supplementary Duty Act, 2012",PARTLY,"The court examined the service code entries and the relevant statutory instruments and found that the petitioner's work is neither the supply of goods nor a procurement service, but a service falling under the code for cleaning, repair and maintenance, for which the deductible tax is ten per cent. Neither the fifteen per cent withheld nor the seven and a half per cent claimed was correct. The rule succeeded only to that extent, and the authority was directed to refund what had been collected above ten per cent.",c02ea5e782bbf88cca212f040ffa78eb70c724803323a658e8533b4176770ac7
