","PETITIONERs are the heirs of a plaintiff whose title suit at Naogaon was decreed by the trial court and then reversed on appeal, the appellate court finding the defendants' version more probable on the documents and the signatures relied on. Two rules were issued, one against each set of defendants, and both arise from the same appellate judgment. RESPONDENTs are the defendants. The question is whether the appellate court erred in reversing the trial court on a question of appreciation of evidence and of the probability of the rival accounts.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court noted that the appellate judge had examined the documents produced about the transfer of land, had noticed that the probate relied on did not appear in the volume book of the sub-registry, and had found discrepancies in the signatures of the attesting witness. On the balance of probabilities the defendants' account appeared the more probable, and the appellate court was entitled to reach that conclusion on the material before it. No error of law having been shown, both rules were brought to an end and the status quo order fell with them.",c2d93e834579da7e650326439c0d64eee4d8cd11e0646a58dfcd3ee166296b91
