Kazi Zinat Hoque, J : 
 
In this application under Article 102 of the Constitution a 
Rule Nisi has been issued calling upon the respondents to show 
cause as to why the impugned provision  stated in the footnote of 
Parishishta Kha of the MPO Guidelines of 2021 which mandates a 
minimum of two departments for MPO enlistment in Bachelor 
Degree (Pass Course) Colleges thereby creating a barrier for the 
petitioners to submit online applications for MPO should not be 
declared ultra vires the Constitution and as to why the respondents 
should not be directed to provide an opportunity for the petitioners 
to submit their online applications for MPO enlistment and 
thereby consider their MPO claims.  
The petitioners, appointed as Lecturers and staff at various 
Non-Government educational institutions applied online for 
enrollment under Monthly Pay Order (MPO) to the Director 
General of the Directorate of Secondary and Higher Secondary 
Education. However, their applications have not been disposed of 
by the respondents. MPO Guidelines of 2010 were replaced by 
MPO Guidelines of 2018 enabling the petitioners to apply for 
MPO. However, the MPO Guidelines of 2021 introduced a 
restriction, mandating a minimum of two departments in Bachelor 
Degree (Pass Course) Colleges for MPO enlistment. 
The petitioners attempted to submit online applications but 
faced difficulties. Despite numerous attempts to contact the 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3
respondents and seek assistance in submitting their applications, 
the petitioners were unsuccessful. Subsequently, they submitted a 
representation dated 08.12.2021, requesting the repeal of the 
restrictive provisions of MPO Guidelines, 2021. 
While their colleagues at the higher secondary level enjoy 
MPO benefits, the respondents took no action to lift the 
restrictions hindering the petitioners from submitting their 
applications. Having received no response the petitioners, through 
their lawyer, issued a notice demanding justice on 09.12.2021. 
Consequently, this writ petition was filed. 
 
Mr. Mohammad Siddique Ullah Miah, with Mr. Md. 
Mahbubur Rashid, learned Advocates appearing for the 
petitioners, argued that the petitioners were appointed long before 
the Monthly Pay Order (MPO) Guidelines, 2021 were framed. 
They were eligible to apply for MPO under MPO Guidelines of 
2010 (as amended in 2013) as well as MPO Guidelines of 2018. 
However, Parishishta Kha of the MPO Guidelines of 2021 created 
an embargo and as a result, the petitioners were unable to submit 
online applications for MPO. Therefore the respondents should be 
directed to lift the embargo so far as the petitioners are concerned. 
 
Mr. Amit Das Gupta, learned Deputy Attorney General with 
Md. Taufiq Sajawar Partho, learned Assistant Attorney General 
appeared for the respondents.  

