Mamnoon Rahman,J: 
 
In an application under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil 
Procedure, 1908 rule was issued calling upon the opposite party Nos. 1-9 
to show cause as to why the impugned Judgment and Order dated 
24.8.2023 and 16.10.2023 passed by the Senior District Judge, 
Moulavibazar in Miscellaneous (Transfer) Case No. 16 of 2023 
admitting the miscellaneous case and calling the record of the Money 
Appeal No. 4 of 2021 and Money Appeal No. 4 of 2022 from the Court 
of the Additional District Judge, 2nd Court, Moulvibazar should not be 
set aside and/or such other or further order or orders passed as to this 
Court may seem fit and proper. 
The learned Advocate for the opposite party No. 1 at the very 
outset submits that considering the delay in the disposal of the Money 
   Present  
          Mr. Justice Mamnoon Rahman 
 
 
       
 
 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2
Appeal No. 4 of 2021 and Money Appeal No. 4 of 2022 he has no 
objection if both the appeals be transferred to any other court for quick 
disposal. 
Considering the facts and circumstances, I am inclined to transfer 
the case to any other court only considering the quick disposal of the 
suit. Accordingly, let the Money Appeal No. 4 of 2021 and Money 
Appeal No. 4 of 2022 be transferred to the court of learned Additional 
District Judge, 2nd Court, Moulvibazar forthwith. The learned Additional 
District Judge, 2nd Court, Moulvibazar is directed to hear and dispose of 
both the appeals strictly on merit by applying its independent and 
judicial mind within 90(ninety) days from the date of receipt of the 
instant order without fail and without giving any adjournment to the 
parties. However, this order shall not affect the disposal of the case on 
merit.  
With this observation and direction, the instant rule is disposed of. 
 
Communicate the order, at once. 
 
           
 
           
 
 
 
 (Mamnoon Rahman,J:)  
 
 
","PETITIONER is a party to two money appeals at Moulvibazar, from 2021 and 2022, which he says are not being disposed of, and he challenges the order by which the District Judge admitted a transfer case and called for their records. RESPONDENT is the other side, which told the court that it had no objection to the appeals being shifted elsewhere provided they were heard quickly. The question is whether the transfer should stand, the petitioner objecting that the records were called for from the court which was already seized of the appeals.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court declined to disturb the transfer. Rather than send the appeals back, it confirmed their transfer to the Additional District Judge, Second Court, at Moulvibazar, and directed that court to hear and decide both appeals strictly on their merits, applying its own judicial mind, within ninety days of receipt of the order and without granting adjournments to either side. The rule was disposed of on that footing, so that the challenge to the transfer order produced no change in the forum.",614fed68de3006b6f4c14441ea1de1b6ac36e2c7a9e2b2a3b5247e3f9d145e56
3900-3940,Criminal Appeal No. 7011 of 2023,2024-05-21,"
