","PETITIONERs are defendants in a title suit of 2006 at Dhaka who asked for an injunction to restrain two co-defendants from putting up construction on the suit land. Their application under Order 39 rules 1 and 2 of the Code was turned down, and nobody appeared before the revisional court to press the rule, which was taken up on the law alone. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The question is whether the refusal rests on sound grounds, the applicants saying that the trial court did not consider their case at all.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the applicants had not made out a prima facie case of title or of possession in their favour, and had not said in the application that they would suffer prejudice if no injunction issued. It further held that an application under section 55 of the Specific Relief Act is not maintainable in a pending suit to restrain another party, the proper course being an application under Order 39. No illegality appearing in the refusal, the rule was brought to an end and the trial court was left to proceed.",9bd9f081684ec9c8d6048e53e42e5c1af58cac6bdb196007bdf93be94fd388b0
