Nurul Abser Chowdhury Vs. Jesmin Akhter (51 DLR (1999) 
352): 
Error in the decision of the subordinate Courts do not by 
itself justify interreference in revision unless it is manifested that 
by the error substantial injustice has been rendered. The decision 
which is calculated to advance substantial justice, through not 
strictly regular, may not be interfered with in revision”.   
Ajab Khan Vs. Karimi Industries and Others (PLD 1980 
Peshwar 259): 
It need hardly be said that the exercise of the powers in 
revision is discretionary and it has been a long-established practice 
in the High Courts that where ""substantial justice has been done 
between the parties, court powers of revision should neither be

<<PAGE 29>>

29
invoked nor exercised. In the case in hand, substantial justice has 
been done by the dismissal of the plaintiff's suit.  
Mst. Ghulam Sakina and 6 Others Vs. Karim Bakhsh (PLD 
1970 Lahore 412): 
In the exercise of its revisional jurisdiction, this Court is not 
bound to interfere in every case even if the order of the lower 
Court is wrong. The exercise of the revisional jurisdiction is 
discretionary and is not to be resorted to when substantial justice 
had been done between the parties.  
Managing Director, Janata Bank Vs, Md Bazlur Rahman 
and Others (51 DLR (AD) (1999) 141: 
In appropriate cases the revisional Court can consider 
additional evidence.  
He next reply to the petitioner argument that: 
a. The petitioner referred a judgment contained in AIR 1982 
SC 816 and stated that the “the first hearing of the suit” is 
when, after the framing of issues, the suit is posted for trial, 
that is, production of evidence. The said expression is also to 
be found in Order 10, rule 1, Order 14, rule 1 (5) and Order 
15, rule 1 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. These 
provisions indicate that “the first hearing of the suit” can 
never be earlier than the date fixed for the preliminary

<<PAGE 30>>

30
examination of the parties. However, in rebuttal of the 
above-mentioned argument we have referred- 
    Shirajul Islam Mollah and Ors. Vs. Bangladesh Bank and 
Ors. reported in 73 DLR 554 case and which stated that- 
   The Court can reject the plaint even before issuance of 
summons. 
b. The petitioner also submits that the two Revisions being 
Civil Revision No. 5470 of 2023 and Civil Revision No. 
5471 of 2023 should not be heard together. In the instant 
scenario it is not possible to see both the cases as isolated 
but have to see together. The plaint submitted by the 
plaintiff-petitioner in filing Miscellaneous Case No. 31 of 
2023 against his mother Kazi Momtaj Shireen used a total of 
24 paragraphs and out of 24 paragraphs they have used the 
name of Kazi Shamsul Haque in 14 paragraphs. As such, 
both the petitions are connected. 
c. The petitioner also submitted that the Hon’ble High Court 
Division does not have any power to consider additional 
evidence except the plaint and the Revisional Application. 
However, we have referred a judgment of Managing 
Director, Janata Bank Vs. Md. Bazlur Rahman and Others 
reported in 51 DLR (AD) 141, where it is stated that in

<<PAGE 31>>

31
appropriate cases the Revisional Court can consider 
additional evidence. Hence, the Hon’ble High Court 
Division has the power to consider additional evidence in 
exercising Revisional power. The Hon’ble High Court 
Division has the power to consider the additional evidences 
e.g. Affidavit of Kazi Momtaj Shireen and G.D filed by  
Kazi Momtaj Shireen and  Kazi Shamsul Haque.   
Heard the learned Advocates for the both parties and 
perused the record. 
The whole Case of the plaintiff-petitioner is that Kazi 
Momtaz Shireen is suffering from mental disorder namely 
depression and dependant personality disorder but in this respect 
the plaintiff-petitioner did not produce any genuine document. So 
there is no reason to believe the plaintiff-petitioner’s case. On the 
other hand from the record it appears that the plaintiff-petitioner 
only filed a prescription to prove his case and for that prescription 
concerned Doctor cannot be prosecuted under Section 195(1)(c) of 
the Code of Criminal Procedure. 
Considering the facts and circumstances of the Case, I find 
no substance in this Rule.

<<PAGE 32>>

32
Accordingly, the Rule is discharged without any order as 
to costs. 
The Judgment and Order dated 11.10.2023 passed by the 
learned District Judge, Dhaka in Miscellaneous Case No. 31 of 
2023 under Section 21 of the Mental Health Act, 2018 rejecting the 
plaint summarily as being not considerable is hereby up-held.  
The order of stay and status-quo granted earlier by this 
Court is hereby vacated. 
Communicate the Judgment to the Courts below at once.

BO-Monir","PETITIONER is a son who filed a case at Dhaka under section 21 of the Mental Health Act, 2018 asking to be appointed guardian of the person and property of his mother, on the footing that she suffers from depression and a dependent personality disorder and cannot look after the shares and properties standing in her name. The District Judge threw the plaint out summarily as not entertainable. RESPONDENT is his full brother, who opposes the move. The question is whether such a case could be shut out at the threshold without any evidence being taken.","Other: Mental Health Act, 2018",REFUSED,"The court observed that the only material produced about the mother's mental condition was a prescription, which proved nothing and could not even be used against the doctor who wrote it under section 195(1)(c) of the Code of Criminal Procedure. No medical record, no assessment and no deposition of any physician was placed on the file, and the allegations about the management of the family companies rested on assertion alone. Additional material sought to be brought in at the revisional stage was declined. There being no substance, the order of the District Judge was confirmed and the interim protection fell away.",328cf0d12f04adb2ed296dc83cbf2a9f897eab530edb13f9c430c09e11091b9c
