","PETITIONER is the seventh defendant in a partition suit at Patuakhali over three decimals of homestead land, who resisted the plaintiff's application for a temporary injunction and won before the Senior Assistant Judge. The District Judge, on appeal, effaced that order and directed both sides to keep the position as it stood, which in effect protected the plaintiff. RESPONDENT is the plaintiff. The defendant says the appellate court put an injunction in place over land whose identity was never settled, leaving him unable to build on his own plot.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court held that an order to maintain the position as it stands operates as an injunction in substance, and that such an order cannot be passed over land that has not been properly identified. It further noted that the plaintiff and the defendant are co-sharers, that a co-sharer in exclusive possession of a defined and separated plot may be protected, but that the plaint did not show the plaintiff to be in such possession of the disputed three decimals. There being no prima facie arguable case, the appellate order could not stand. It was effaced, the order of the trial court was restored, and the suit was to be finished in six months.",1093820f1475c55b9bf55e8c28e46ea0d9a192ab82f355b6ae8ded11a3d525ff
