","PETITIONERs are defendants in a title suit at Madaripur over 42.83 decimals of land whose names appear in the latest record, and who are putting up a building on the plot. The Senior Assistant Judge declined the plaintiffs' application for a temporary injunction; the Additional District Judge reversed that and restrained them. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs, who say the record in the defendants' favour is wrong. The defendants ask that the appellate order be removed, pointing to the loss a halt in construction would cause them.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court did not decide the question of title, holding that it could not be settled without evidence in a case where both sides are co-sharers and both are on the land. Instead of answering the rule one way or the other, it directed the parties to keep the possession and the position of the land as they are, permitted the defendants to carry on with the construction at their own risk, and made them liable to pull the structure down if the trial court so ordered. The suit was to be finished within six months, with consecutive dates and no unnecessary adjournments.",b2c0cfcf149815a41e45f680ee052e07e0b8dc44822282bb6075aba0442cb06b
