","PETITIONERs are defendants in a title suit at Kushtia which was decided against the plaintiffs, who carried the matter in appeal. Before the appellate court, two of the respondents sought to have their written statement taken on record so that their claim under a deed of 1994 could be considered; that application was turned down. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants say the appeal cannot be decided properly on the title while a party claiming under a registered deed is left entirely unheard.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court observed that the respondents who sought to put in the written statement had been impleaded in the appeal as principal respondents and that their case rested on a registered deed of January 1994, which on their account was executed in favour of their father. A decision on the appeal without their pleading on record would leave the question of title incompletely examined and would multiply proceedings. The appellate judge ought therefore to have taken the written statement on the file, and his refusal was without foundation. The rule was answered in the petitioners' favour and the appeal was to be finished in six months.",bf10af2ef46f94796074da8c38fb6d04bbb7c1d363dc165f541ce62942dffbc9
