                                           Judgment on 20.05.2024. 
Sheikh Abdul Awal, J: 
This criminal appeal at the instance of convict 
appellant, Md. Anarul Islam is directed against the 
impugned judgment and order of conviction and Sentence 
dated 27.09.2017 passed by the learned Sessions Judge, 
Rangpur in Sessions Case No. 960 of 2016 arising out of 
G.R. No. 125 of 2014 corresponding to Mithapukur 
Police Station Case No. 30 dated 24.03.2014 convicting 
the accused appellant under Section 22(Ga) of the 
Madok  Drabbya Niyontron Ain, 1990  and sentencing him 
thereunder to suffer rigorous  imprisonment for a period 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2
of 02(two) years and to pay a fine of Taka 10,000/-(ten 
thousand) in default to suffer simple imprisonment for 
03 (three) months more.  
No one appears for the convict appellant. 
In view of the fact that this petty old criminal 
Appeal arising out of 02(two) years sentence has been 
dragging before this Court for more than 5 years, I am 
inclined to dispose of the same. 
At the time of pronouncement of judgment, it 
appears from the record that this Court on 06.11.2018 at 
the time of admission of appeal granted ad-interim bail 
to the accused appellant for a period of 01(one) year and 
thereafter, no one took any steps to extend the order of 
bail, as a result of which the said bail was expired long 
before on 06.11.2019. In that view of the matter, I have 
no hesitation to hold that the convict appellant is a 
fugitive from law and justice. 
In the case of Anti-Corruption Commission Vs. Dr. 
HBM Iqbal Alamgir, reported in 15 BLC(AD) 44, it has 
been held that the Court would not act in aid of an 
accused person, who is a fugitive from law and justice. 
In view of the above, this Appeal must fail. 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3
In the result, the appeal is dismissed. Since the 
appeal is dismissed the convict- appellant, Md. Anarul 
Islam is directed to surrender his bail bond within 3 
(three) months from today to suffer his sentence, failing 
which the Trial Court below shall take necessary steps to 
secure arrest against the convict appellant.  
 
Send down the lower Court records at once. 
 
","PETITIONER was convicted at Rangpur in 2016 under section 22(Ga) of the Narcotics Control Act, 1990 and sentenced to two years with a fine of Tk. 10,000, in a case that began at Mithapukur in 2014. He appealed in 2018 and was given ad-interim bail for a year at admission, which nobody asked to have extended. RESPONDENT is the state. When the appeal came up for hearing nobody appeared for him, and the court was inclined to dispose of it on the record since the sentence involved was short and the case had been dragging for more than five years.","Other: Narcotics Control Act, 1990",REFUSED,"The court noted that the bail given at admission had lapsed in November 2019 and that no step had been taken since to have it renewed, so the appellant was not available to suffer the sentence and must be treated as one keeping out of the way of the law. On the principle that a court will not act in aid of a person in that position, the appeal could not be heard on the merits in his absence. It was therefore turned away, with a direction that he surrender within three months, failing which the trial court was to secure his arrest.",955cc99f4bfa60129955201d693293b1c4682fcd0283d1e09ed92ebb4c7eb36b
